\title{Antipodality of Hypercube Vertex Sets via the\\ Opposite-Semicube Helly Property}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study finite sets of vertices of the $n$-dimensional hypercube $Q_n$, whose
vertices we identify with bit-vectors in $\{0,1\}^n$. A vertex set $S$ is
\emph{antipodal} if it is closed under the bit-complement (antipode) map. For each
coordinate $i$ and bit $b$, the \emph{semicube} $S_i^b$ collects the vertices of
$S$ whose $i$-th coordinate equals $b$; the pair $S_i^0, S_i^1$ are \emph{opposite
semicubes}. We prove a complete characterization: under the Helly property for its
semicubes, a finite vertex set is antipodal if and only if all of its opposite
semicubes are isometrically isomorphic with respect to the Hamming metric. The
forward direction exhibits the antipode map as a canonical isometric isomorphism
between opposite semicubes. The converse is proved without invoking antipodality:
it constructs the antipode of each vertex as the unique common solution of a
family of coordinate constraints, using a standalone Helly-number-$2$ theorem for
hypercube semicubes together with a cardinality-balance argument that guarantees
pairwise satisfiability. We also isolate two results of independent interest: the
antipode map is a global Hamming isometry, and the semicubes of the full hypercube
satisfy the Helly property with Helly number exactly two. All results hold in every
dimension $n$.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The hypercube $Q_n$ is among the most fundamental objects in discrete
mathematics. Its vertex set is $\{0,1\}^n$, and two vertices are adjacent exactly
when their bit-vectors differ in a single coordinate; the resulting graph metric
is the \emph{Hamming distance}. Sitting inside $Q_n$ is a distinguished global
symmetry, the \emph{antipode} or bit-complement map, which flips every coordinate
and sends each vertex to the unique vertex at maximal distance $n$ from it.

A finite vertex set $S \subseteq \{0,1\}^n$ is \emph{antipodal} when it is closed
under this map. Antipodal sets are highly symmetric configurations that arise
naturally in coding theory (codes closed under complementation), in the theory of
partial cubes and median graphs, and in the combinatorics of the cube. The
purpose of this paper is to characterize antipodality through \emph{local} data,
replacing a single global closure condition by a family of independent
direction-by-direction comparisons.

The local data are the \emph{semicubes}. Fixing a coordinate $i$ partitions $S$
into two halves according to the value of the $i$-th bit; these halves, $S_i^0$
and $S_i^1$, are the opposite semicubes in direction $i$. Semicubes are the
discrete analogue of halfspaces, and comparing the two opposite semicubes in each
direction is a natural probe of the internal symmetry of $S$.

Our main result states that this probe is complete, provided the semicubes obey a
Helly-type principle (Theorem~\ref{thm:main}). The forward implication is direct:
antipodality supplies the antipode map as a canonical isometry between opposite
halves. The converse is the substantive direction and is proved constructively and
without circularity: assuming only that opposite semicubes are isometric plus the
Helly property, we build the antipode of each vertex and show it lies in $S$.

The bridge between local and global is a classical convexity principle. Eduard
Helly's theorem upgrades pairwise intersection to total intersection for
sufficiently convex families. We prove a discrete counterpart for hypercube
semicubes with Helly number exactly two, and use it as the engine of the converse.

\section{Definitions and setup}

Throughout, fix a dimension $n \in \mathbb{N}$.

\begin{definition}[Vertices]
We identify the vertices of $Q_n$ with functions $v : \{0,\dots,n-1\} \to
\{0,1\}$, i.e.\ bit-vectors of length $n$. We write $V_n = \{0,1\}^n$ for the
vertex set.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Bit complement and antipode]
The complement on a single bit is $\anti{0} = 1$ and $\anti{1} = 0$; it is a
fixed-point-free involution. The \emph{antipode} of $v \in V_n$ is $\anti{v}$ with
$(\anti{v})_i = \anti{v_i}$. It is an injective involution.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Hamming distance]
For $u,v \in V_n$, $\Ham(u,v) = \#\{ i : u_i \neq v_i \}$ is the number of
coordinates in which they differ.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Semicubes]
For a finite $S \subseteq V_n$, a coordinate $i$, and a bit $b$, the
\emph{semicube} is $S_i^b = \{ v \in S : v_i = b \}$. The pair $S_i^0, S_i^1$ are
the \emph{opposite semicubes} at $i$; they partition $S$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Isometric isomorphism]
Two finite sets $A, B \subseteq V_n$ are \emph{isometrically isomorphic},
$A \cong B$, if some $f : V_n \to V_n$ restricts to a bijection $A \to B$ and
satisfies $\Ham(f(x),f(y)) = \Ham(x,y)$ for all $x,y \in A$. In particular
$A \cong B$ implies $|A| = |B|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Antipodality]
$S$ is \emph{antipodal} if $v \in S \implies \anti{v} \in S$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Helly property]
The semicubes of $S$ satisfy the \emph{Helly property} if, for every finite family
$F$ of coordinate constraints $(i,b) \in \{0,\dots,n-1\} \times \{0,1\}$,
individual satisfiability
$\bigl(\forall p \in F,\ \exists x \in S,\ x_{p_1} = p_2\bigr)$ together with
pairwise joint satisfiability
$\bigl(\forall p,q \in F,\ \exists x \in S,\ x_{p_1}=p_2 \wedge x_{q_1}=q_2\bigr)$
imply global joint satisfiability
$\bigl(\exists x \in S,\ \forall p \in F,\ x_{p_1} = p_2\bigr)$. This is Helly
number $2$.
\end{definition}

\section{The antipode is a global isometry}

\begin{lemma}[Antipode preserves distance]\label{lem:iso}
For all $u,v \in V_n$, $\Ham(\anti{u}, \anti{v}) = \Ham(u,v)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Bit complement is a bijection, so $u_i \neq v_i \iff \anti{u_i} \neq \anti{v_i}$.
The differing-coordinate sets of $(u,v)$ and $(\anti{u},\anti{v})$ coincide, hence
have equal cardinality.
\end{proof}

Since $\anti{\anti{v}} = v$, the antipode is a self-inverse global isometry that
swaps opposite semicubes: $v_i = 0 \iff (\anti{v})_i = 1$.

\section{Forward direction}

\begin{theorem}[Forward direction]\label{thm:forward}
If $S$ is antipodal, then for every coordinate $i$ the antipode map restricts to
an isometric isomorphism $S_i^0 \cong S_i^1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $f(v) = \anti{v}$. If $v \in S_i^0$ then $\anti{v} \in S$ (antipodality) and
$(\anti{v})_i = 1$, so $\anti{v} \in S_i^1$. The antipode is injective; given
$w \in S_i^1$, $\anti{w} \in S_i^0$ and $f(\anti{w}) = w$, giving surjectivity.
Isometry is Lemma~\ref{lem:iso}.
\end{proof}

\section{Helly for hypercube semicubes}

\begin{theorem}[Helly for semicubes]\label{thm:helly}
Let $F$ be a finite family of coordinate constraints. If every two constraints in
$F$ are jointly satisfiable in $V_n$, then all of $F$ is jointly satisfiable in
$V_n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Pairwise satisfiability forbids two constraints fixing the same coordinate to
different bits. Hence the map sending each mentioned coordinate to its demanded
bit is well defined; extending it arbitrarily (say by $0$) yields a vertex
satisfying all of $F$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The Helly number is exactly $2$: singletons are always satisfiable, yet a clash
such as $\{(i,0),(i,1)\}$ is detectable only by checking pairs.
\end{remark}

\section{Cardinality balance and pairwise flips}

\begin{lemma}[Isometry equalizes cardinality]\label{lem:card}
If $A \cong B$ then $|A| = |B|$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{corollary}[Balance]\label{cor:balance}
If $S_i^0 \cong S_i^1$ for every $i$, then $|S_i^0| = |S_i^1|$ for every $i$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{lemma}[Pairwise flip from balance]\label{lem:flip}
Suppose $|S_i^0| = |S_i^1|$ for every coordinate $i$. Then for every $v \in S$ and
every pair of coordinates $i,j$ there exists $w \in S$ with $w_i = \anti{v_i}$ and
$w_j = \anti{v_j}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If $i = j$, balance forces the semicube opposite to $v$'s in direction $i$ to be
nonempty. If $i \neq j$, partition $S$ into quadrants by coordinates $i,j$ with
counts $N(c,d) = \#\{x \in S : x_i = c,\ x_j = d\}$. The marginals
$N(c,d) + N(c,\anti{d}) = |S_i^c|$ and $N(c,d) + N(\anti{c},d) = |S_j^d|$ together
with the balances yield $N(c,d) = N(\anti{c},\anti{d})$. Since the quadrant of $v$
has $N(v_i,v_j) \geq 1$, so does $N(\anti{v_i},\anti{v_j})$, providing $w$.
\end{proof}

\section{Converse direction}

\begin{theorem}[Converse direction]\label{thm:converse}
If the semicubes of $S$ satisfy the Helly property and $S_i^0 \cong S_i^1$ for
every $i$, then $S$ is antipodal.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Fix $v \in S$ and let $F = \{ (i, \anti{v_i}) : i \}$. A vertex $x \in S$ satisfies
all of $F$ iff $x = \anti{v}$. By Corollary~\ref{cor:balance} the opposite
semicubes are balanced, so Lemma~\ref{lem:flip} makes $F$ individually and
pairwise satisfiable inside $S$. The Helly property yields $x \in S$ satisfying all
of $F$, i.e.\ $\anti{v} \in S$. The construction never assumes antipodality.
\end{proof}

\section{The characterization}

\begin{theorem}[Antipodality characterization]\label{thm:main}
Let $S$ be a finite vertex set of $Q_n$ whose semicubes satisfy the Helly property.
Then $S$ is antipodal if and only if $S_i^0 \cong S_i^1$ for every coordinate $i$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine Theorem~\ref{thm:forward} and Theorem~\ref{thm:converse}.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{Algorithm A (antipodality by opposite-semicube comparison).} For each
coordinate $i$, form $S_i^0, S_i^1$ and test isometric isomorphism; report
antipodal iff all pass. A fast necessary screen is cardinality balance
$|S_i^0| = |S_i^1|$ for all $i$, computable in one pass; the canonical witness
reduces to checking closure under complement.

\textbf{Algorithm B (antipode construction via Helly).} For $v \in S$ with balanced
semicubes, satisfy the flip constraints $(i, \anti{v_i})$ using guaranteed
pairwise flips and the Helly upgrade to obtain the global witness $\anti{v} \in S$.

With $|S| = m$ and dimension $n$, forming semicubes and balances costs $O(mn)$;
the canonical (antipode) witness collapses the isometry test to an $O(mn)$
membership check for closure under complement.

\section{Applications and discussion}

Semicubes are the halfspaces of partial-cube theory, so the characterization
places antipodality in the halfspace/Helly-number framework, with the antipodal
case as the extremal instance where every halfspace has an exact opposite. In
coding theory, complementation-closed (antipodal) codes are certified from local
slice comparisons, enabling parallel verification. Conceptually, the result is a
local-to-global principle: a global involutive symmetry is detected by independent
per-direction checks, with Helly's principle as the bridge. The converse is
constructive and non-circular, manufacturing each antipode from cardinality
balance and the Helly upgrade; the Helly number for hypercube semicubes is exactly
two, mirroring the classical Helly number of intervals on a line.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Abstract-isomorphism Helly converse]
A finite partial cube satisfying the opposite-semicube Helly property is antipodal
whenever all of its opposite semicubes are abstractly isomorphic (matching
cardinalities and internal geometry, with no preferred matching supplied). The key
insight is that abstract isomorphism supplies, per direction, some
distance-preserving matching, and the Helly property is the coherence condition
forcing these to coincide with the global antipode involution.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Helly number is a partial-cube invariant]
For every finite partial cube, the Helly number of its family of semicubes is at
most two, with equality unless the cube is a single vertex.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Diameter rigidity]
A finite partial cube is antipodal if and only if every vertex has a unique vertex
at maximum distance and this correspondence is a distance-preserving involution.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}

We characterized antipodality of finite hypercube vertex sets through a local
comparison of opposite semicubes, bridged by a Helly-number-two principle. The
forward direction exhibits the antipode as a canonical isometry; the converse
constructs antipodes from cardinality balance and Helly's principle without
circularity. The antipode map is a global isometry, and hypercube semicubes have
Helly number exactly two.

